On Friday, March 21, 2014 10:46:48 AM UTC-4, Jane Foster wrote:
> Now the code that I have modified to try to apply the Matt Trick using a mean vector zero and correlation matrix R, as described in the FAQ. I've commented out lines that I removed, and added lines are in red text. Sorry for the mess!

I think you have substituted the univariate Matt trick (which presumes a diagonal covariance matrix) for a model with a dense covariance matrix,  where the multivariate Matt trick would be appropriate. And there are some other things you could do as well that would help. I think your .stan file should be

data {
    int<lower=0> N;
    int<lower=0> J;
    int<lower=0> K;
    vector[N] y;
    matrix<lower=0, upper=1>[N,K] X;
    int county[N];
}
parameters {

    vector[K] e_B[J];
    vector[K] mu_B;

    corr_matrix[K] R;
    vector<lower=0,upper=100>[K] tau //standard deviations

    real<lower=0> sigma_y;
}
transformed parameters {

   vector[K] B[J];
   {
       matrix[K,K] L;
       L <- diag_matrix(tau) * cholesky_decompose(R);
       for (j in 1:J) B[j] <- mu_B + L * e_B[j];
   }
}
model {
    vector[N] mu_y;
    mu_B ~ normal(0,100);
    //implicit: R ~ lkj_corr(1);
    //implicit: tau ~ uniform(0,100);
    //implicit: sigma_y is positive but improper

    //implies B[j] ~ multi_normal(mu_B, diag_matrix(tau) * R * diag_matrix(tau)
    for (j in 1:J) e_B[j] ~ normal(0, 1);
    for (n in 1:N) mu_y[n] <- dot_product(B[county[n]], X[n]);
    y ~ normal(mu_y, sigma_y);
}

It would be better still to use the onion method that I posted on a couple of other threads so as to avoid taking the Cholesky decomposition of R, but one step at a time.

Ben

I think the problem is with your definitions of prior distributions. If I have understood correctly, you would like to have
beta_mu[d] ~ normal(0,100)
and then for the components of beta[s] vector
beta[s,d] ~ normal(beta_mu[d], beta_sigma[d])

Right?

So I think your current code should be changed to:
 for (d in 1:nEV){
    beta_mu[d] ~ normal(0,100);
    for (s in 1:nS)
      beta[s,d] ~ normal(beta_mu[d],beta_sigma[d]);
 }

Further, we could make use of vectorization in Stan and drop the outer loop, ie:
    beta_mu ~ normal(0,100);
    for (s in 1:nS)
      beta[s] ~ normal(beta_mu,beta_sigma);

This gives the following results in 10s or so:
  mean se_mean   sd   2.5%    25%    50%    75%  97.5% n_eff Rhat
beta_mu[1]       1.3     0.0  0.5    0.4    0.9    1.2    1.6    2.1   399    1
beta_mu[2]       3.4     0.0  0.6    2.4    3.0    3.3    3.7    4.6   151    1
beta_mu[3]      -4.9     0.1  0.7   -6.4   -5.4   -4.9   -4.4   -3.7   138    1
beta_sigma[1]    2.9     0.0  0.5    2.1    2.6    2.9    3.2    3.9   132    1
beta_sigma[2]    2.8     0.0  0.5    1.8    2.4    2.7    3.1    3.9   126    1
beta_sigma[3]    3.3     0.0  0.6    2.3    2.8    3.2    3.7    4.6   149    1

For an ideal point model (for simplicity, call it IRT) I’m working on, I’d like to let discrimination be positive or negative, since unpooled analyses I’ve run show that some discriminations do seem to take on different signs in different countries, and I don’t want to assume that my reverse coding will always be right.

In ARM, it’s recommended to add a predictor x coding one person (or item) as positive, the other negative, the rest 0, and estimate an orienting parameter d such that discrimination[i] = x[i]*d+eta[i].

I gave this approach a shot in Stan (checking it against the implementation in the ARM example posted at github), and the models are failing to converge after long, long runs, and often not even generating estimated discriminations of opposite signs for the items chosen! (I chose those items both because of prior knowledge about the nature of the items and because a standard IRT analysis using ltm shows them to be the items with the most positive and negative discriminations, respectively).

I’ve also experimented with constraining the priors, using a loop in the model section specifying:

